1
00:00:09,634 --> 00:00:10,676
Jane?

2
00:00:16,349 --> 00:00:17,892
- Hi, Lisbon.
- What are you doing?

3
00:00:18,392 --> 00:00:19,560
just working.

4
00:00:20,269 --> 00:00:23,272
Okay, well, out here in the real world
we have a case. We have to go.

5
00:00:23,481 --> 00:00:24,565
Yeah. Okay.

6
00:00:43,417 --> 00:00:44,460
Let's go.

7
00:00:45,836 --> 00:00:49,090
You think you're so mysterious.
I know what you're doing in there.

8
00:00:49,549 --> 00:00:50,591
Oh, really? What?

9
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,177
You're making a list
trying to figure out who it is

10
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,721
- that you've met that's Red John.
- Not just met, shaken hands with.

11
00:00:55,846 --> 00:00:57,265
How many people
are on that list, anyway?

12
00:00:57,390 --> 00:00:58,432
A lot.

13
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:02,186
Got a male shooting victim.
Looks to be early 20s.

14
00:01:02,478 --> 00:01:04,021
Happened about an hour ago.
No I.D.

15
00:01:04,188 --> 00:01:06,774
- Who called it in?
- Bartender. We're getting a statement.

16
00:01:06,899 --> 00:01:09,777
- What do the witnesses have to say?
- Not sure. Cho's talking to them.

17
00:01:10,111 --> 00:01:12,280
- Has the coroner taken a look at him yet?
- Yeah, he's on his way.

18
00:01:12,572 --> 00:01:14,532
What... what's that on his face?

19
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:16,117
- What?
- Oh, boy.

20
00:01:18,744 --> 00:01:20,413
Happy anniversary!

21
00:01:21,956 --> 00:01:23,916
- What's goin' on?
- Come in, everybody!

22
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:26,711
Ladies and gentlemen

23
00:01:27,336 --> 00:01:30,881
ten years ago, Teresa Lisbon
left the San Francisco Police Department

24
00:01:31,132 --> 00:01:33,175
to join the California Bureau
of Investigation.

25
00:01:33,759 --> 00:01:35,136
Today she is without doubt

26
00:01:35,344 --> 00:01:37,847
the rootinest, tootinest,
sharp-shootinest cop

27
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:39,432
in the whole state
of California.

28
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:42,727
- Happy anniversary, boss.
- Hear, hear.

29
00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:45,730
So if we could start the music, please?

30
00:01:45,896 --> 00:01:47,481
- I had no idea.
- Let's party!

31
00:01:47,607 --> 00:01:49,984
- Congratulations, Lisbon.
- Thank you, Ron.

32
00:01:50,276 --> 00:01:52,653
- Why did you do this?
- It was Rigsby's idea.

33
00:01:54,363 --> 00:01:55,406
Yeah?

34
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:57,867
- Is he taking his clothes off?
- He is.

35
00:01:57,992 --> 00:01:59,035
Oh, my God.

36
00:02:00,328 --> 00:02:02,538
- Congratulations.
- Thank you, Ray.

37
00:02:02,663 --> 00:02:04,040
Haffner. Good to see you.

38
00:02:04,165 --> 00:02:05,541
You know, I really enjoyed
when we worked together.

39
00:02:05,666 --> 00:02:07,335
- We should do it again sometime.
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

40
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:08,836
You mean when you
almost got me fired?

41
00:02:09,045 --> 00:02:11,714
Yeah, that was a lot of fun.
Hey, can we grab lunch?

42
00:02:11,839 --> 00:02:13,215
There's just something
I wanna discuss with you.

43
00:02:13,341 --> 00:02:14,383
Yeah, sure.

44
00:02:14,508 --> 00:02:15,760
All right. I'll call you.
Jane.

45
00:02:16,636 --> 00:02:19,013
How come he doesn't like me?
You got lunch here, you got...

46
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:20,765
- Oh, look out.
- What?

47
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:22,600
Okay, that's enough.
Shoo. Okay?

48
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:24,143
It was fun.
But not my butt.

49
00:02:25,978 --> 00:02:28,230
That was the office.
Homicide out in Placer County.

50
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:30,358
The team that was supposed
to cover for us today

51
00:02:30,483 --> 00:02:31,525
can't make it out there.

52
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:34,570
Well, I don't think we can drag Lisbon
away from this.

53
00:02:34,695 --> 00:02:36,906
- Can't we cover it, Cho?
- Yeah. Yeah, we can.

54
00:02:37,031 --> 00:02:40,034
Okay. Where did you
find that guy?

55
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:42,662
That was fun.
But we're done, okay?

56
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:44,121
Oh, yeah.
Those are nice moves.

57
00:02:51,253 --> 00:02:53,339
CBI. I'm Agent Cho,
this is Patrick Jane.

58
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:56,592
Rick Anaya. Chief here in Elliston.
Good to meet you. This way.

59
00:02:57,301 --> 00:02:58,928
The bodies were found
in a secret basement

60
00:02:59,053 --> 00:03:00,096
under the barn floor.

61
00:03:00,846 --> 00:03:02,264
And what's with
the secret basement?

62
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:05,184
Yeah, probably built in the '20s
to hide booze during prohibition.

63
00:03:05,893 --> 00:03:07,186
My dad, who was chief before me

64
00:03:07,311 --> 00:03:09,188
said there used to be stills
all through these parts.

65
00:03:09,897 --> 00:03:11,065
Kids were playing
in the barn

66
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:13,818
found a trap door,
went in, found some bones.

67
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:18,906
If you boys are still in town for dinner
Ella's Diner on Allen street...

68
00:03:19,365 --> 00:03:20,700
best damn chili in town.

69
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:27,123
What's the coroner say?

70
00:03:27,331 --> 00:03:30,126
Well, right now he's guessing
they died 20, 25 years ago.

71
00:03:31,168 --> 00:03:32,712
He confirms three bodies in all.

72
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:38,175
What is it?

73
00:03:39,427 --> 00:03:40,761
looks like
a shotgun pellet.

74
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:42,596
Yeah, we found some of those
under the bones.

75
00:03:43,097 --> 00:03:45,599
Figured they fell out the body
as they decomposed.

76
00:03:46,183 --> 00:03:47,560
No shell casings in here, though.

77
00:03:47,768 --> 00:03:50,771
Must have shot them somewhere else
brought the bodies here.

78
00:03:52,857 --> 00:03:54,900
- Anything else?
- Yeah, a couple of things.

79
00:03:56,277 --> 00:03:58,612
High school class ring.
Guess it fell off one of them.

80
00:04:00,197 --> 00:04:04,034
- We'll check that out. Is that it?
- No, this .38.

81
00:04:04,618 --> 00:04:06,036
And one round's been fired.

82
00:04:06,412 --> 00:04:07,788
Not in here, as far as we can tell.

83
00:04:09,749 --> 00:04:10,791
You see that wire?

84
00:04:12,376 --> 00:04:13,419
On the wrist?

85
00:04:13,586 --> 00:04:16,005
See, I'm guessing at least two of the victims
were tied up with it.

86
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:18,674
This is very depressing.
I'm gonna wait outside.

87
00:05:27,983 --> 00:05:29,943
The Red John symbol
was on the side of the barn?

88
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:31,278
- That's weird.
- Yeah.

89
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:34,198
Must have been there
15, 20 years.

90
00:05:34,364 --> 00:05:36,784
Wonder what it means.
What did Jane say?

91
00:05:36,992 --> 00:05:38,410
Nothing.
Wouldn't talk about it.

92
00:05:38,786 --> 00:05:41,705
Okay.
Everybody feel as bad as I do?

93
00:05:42,539 --> 00:05:43,707
Not if you feel
like you look.

94
00:05:44,041 --> 00:05:45,083
I feel fine.

95
00:05:45,209 --> 00:05:46,627
What time did you get back
to the party last night?

96
00:05:46,877 --> 00:05:48,545
A little past 10:00.
Not that you noticed.

97
00:05:48,796 --> 00:05:51,507
Yeah. That, Tequila
was a killer.

98
00:05:54,927 --> 00:05:57,346
{\an8}Did I,... Do anything embarrassing?

99
00:05:57,679 --> 00:05:59,181
- Yes.
- Really? What?

100
00:05:59,431 --> 00:06:01,183
{\an8}Good morning.
Thank you for the party.

101
00:06:01,475 --> 00:06:03,560
{\an8}- It was very thoughtful.
- You're welcome.

102
00:06:03,977 --> 00:06:06,146
But I am never gonna forgive you
for that stripper.

103
00:06:07,439 --> 00:06:08,816
Where are we
with the three victims?

104
00:06:09,107 --> 00:06:12,236
{\an8}Coroner says they probably died
25 years ago, so 1988.

105
00:06:12,444 --> 00:06:13,862
Talked to the ring manufacturer.

106
00:06:13,987 --> 00:06:16,365
{\an8}They traced it back
to a Lester Bradovich

107
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:17,950
{\an8}who went to a high school in Dixon.

108
00:06:18,158 --> 00:06:19,201
Checking him out now.

109
00:06:19,493 --> 00:06:21,787
The gun that was found near
the bodies isn't registered.

110
00:06:21,954 --> 00:06:23,330
{\an8}No sign of the bullet
that was fired, either.

111
00:06:23,497 --> 00:06:25,290
{\an8}But I've got techs sweeping the area
around the barn.

112
00:06:25,457 --> 00:06:27,417
Nice work. Let me know what you find.

113
00:06:28,794 --> 00:06:29,837
{\an8}She was kidding, right?

114
00:06:30,212 --> 00:06:31,421
{\an8}- About never forgiving?
- Nope.

115
00:06:35,467 --> 00:06:36,510
{\an8}How was the rest of the party?

116
00:06:36,885 --> 00:06:39,137
{\an8}Torture. That can never,
ever happen again.

117
00:06:39,638 --> 00:06:41,807
{\an8}- Ten years, huh?
- More, actually.

118
00:06:42,015 --> 00:06:43,392
{\an8}Every year with you
counts as two.

119
00:06:44,601 --> 00:06:46,854
{\an8}- Are those from the crime scene?
- Yes.

120
00:06:53,861 --> 00:06:56,238
- This can't be a coincidence, can it?
- There's no such thing.

121
00:06:57,155 --> 00:06:59,992
{\an8}These murders happened ten years
before Red John was even active.

122
00:07:00,117 --> 00:07:02,035
{\an8}A shotgun was never
his weapon of choice.

123
00:07:02,202 --> 00:07:03,245
{\an8}True.

124
00:07:03,453 --> 00:07:04,997
{\an8}Well, what happened?
How'd it get there?

125
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,545
{\an8}They're excellent questions.
You know where to find me.

126
00:07:16,466 --> 00:07:17,759
{\an8}Got a hit on Lester Bradovich.

127
00:07:18,385 --> 00:07:19,636
He had a couple of arrests
in the mid-'80s.

128
00:07:19,803 --> 00:07:22,139
{\an8}Small stuff. Possession,
drunk and disorderly.

129
00:07:22,723 --> 00:07:24,224
{\an8}No record of him
after 1987.

130
00:07:24,433 --> 00:07:26,101
- Family?
- A brother in town

131
00:07:26,226 --> 00:07:27,269
but here's the weird thing...

132
00:07:27,394 --> 00:07:28,937
nobody ever filed a missing
persons report on him.

133
00:07:29,271 --> 00:07:31,982
{\an8}That's odd. You and Rigsby go
and see what the brother has to say.

134
00:07:32,190 --> 00:07:33,233
{\an8}All right.

135
00:07:35,193 --> 00:07:37,779
{\an8}So what did I do last night?
Come on. I'm dying here.

136
00:07:39,072 --> 00:07:40,991
{\an8}You got up on the table and sang
"Bohemian Rhapsody."

137
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:43,744
{\an8}I do a pretty good
Freddie Mercury.

138
00:07:44,077 --> 00:07:45,120
{\an8}That's not so embarrassing.

139
00:07:45,329 --> 00:07:46,371
{\an8}Maybe not for you.

140
00:07:47,164 --> 00:07:48,707
{\an8}- Gordon Bradovich?
- Yeah?

141
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:51,835
We need to talk to you
about your brother Lester.

142
00:07:52,169 --> 00:07:54,129
Lester? Are you serious?

143
00:07:57,215 --> 00:07:59,384
{\an8}You're...you're positive
it's Lester?

144
00:07:59,635 --> 00:08:01,553
{\an8}We're waiting for final confirmation
from the coroner

145
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:03,013
{\an8}but we're pretty sure, yeah.

146
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:06,099
{\an8}This is just unreal.

147
00:08:06,516 --> 00:08:07,893
When was your last contact
with your brother?

148
00:08:09,311 --> 00:08:11,813
{\an8}Oh, God, sometime
in the late '80s.

149
00:08:12,022 --> 00:08:13,440
{\an8}So you haven't seen him
in almost 25 years

150
00:08:13,607 --> 00:08:15,067
{\an8}but you never reported him missing.
Why is that?

151
00:08:15,275 --> 00:08:17,986
- We didn't think he was missing.
- I don't understand.

152
00:08:18,737 --> 00:08:21,239
{\an8}Lester had been
in a lot of trouble.

153
00:08:21,907 --> 00:08:24,409
{\an8}Drugs and scrapes
with the law, and...

154
00:08:25,243 --> 00:08:27,120
then, when he was about 24

155
00:08:27,245 --> 00:08:29,539
he...he joined with this,
this group.

156
00:08:30,123 --> 00:08:33,001
A little weird, but very strict,
very disciplined.

157
00:08:33,669 --> 00:08:34,836
Seemed to straighten him out.

158
00:08:35,003 --> 00:08:37,381
He, he got calmer,
stopped getting into trouble.

159
00:08:37,756 --> 00:08:39,549
And what does that have to do
with his disappearance?

160
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:43,261
Well, after about a year with them
he, he came to my dad and me

161
00:08:43,387 --> 00:08:46,807
and he said that he had
to break off all contact

162
00:08:46,932 --> 00:08:48,558
with his biological family.

163
00:08:49,893 --> 00:08:52,020
Yeah. His group said
that this was the only way

164
00:08:52,145 --> 00:08:54,064
his true identity could emerge.

165
00:08:55,148 --> 00:08:56,191
We were pretty hurt.

166
00:08:56,650 --> 00:08:59,736
But Lester...he was totally
matter-of-fact about it had to be done.

167
00:09:01,238 --> 00:09:04,324
That was...
the last time I talked to him.

168
00:09:05,575 --> 00:09:07,452
Now you're telling me
he's been dead all this time?

169
00:09:07,619 --> 00:09:09,830
I just...I-I don't even know
what to think.

170
00:09:10,414 --> 00:09:12,374
- What's the name of the group?
- Vision.

171
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:16,044
No. Visualize.

172
00:09:18,338 --> 00:09:19,381
You've heard of it?

173
00:09:24,386 --> 00:09:25,595
- Hello?
- "Teresa."

174
00:09:25,846 --> 00:09:28,807
- Ray Haffner. I've been looking for you.
- Hey, Ray. What's up?

175
00:09:29,266 --> 00:09:31,560
I was wondering if we could
snag that lunch today

176
00:09:31,685 --> 00:09:33,520
at 1:00 at the State House Cafe.

177
00:09:33,687 --> 00:09:35,981
Sure. You wanna tell me
what this is about? I'm intrigued.

178
00:09:36,356 --> 00:09:38,191
Then my plan is working.
I'll see you then.

179
00:09:38,942 --> 00:09:40,986
- What does he want?
- Lunch.

180
00:09:41,695 --> 00:09:43,196
Mr. Jane. Agent Lisbon.

181
00:09:43,488 --> 00:09:45,115
Good to see you again.
Jason Cooper.

182
00:09:45,323 --> 00:09:46,658
Brother Cooper.

183
00:09:47,576 --> 00:09:51,997
Last time, I saw you was, when Bret Stiles
had just uncovered your plan

184
00:09:52,122 --> 00:09:53,165
to take over this place.

185
00:09:53,290 --> 00:09:54,791
I thought he would have
gutted you like a trout

186
00:09:54,916 --> 00:09:55,959
but here you are.

187
00:09:56,084 --> 00:09:59,129
Bret's generosity is only exceeded
by his capacity for forgiveness.

188
00:09:59,796 --> 00:10:01,715
Is Mr. stiles here?
We need to speak with him.

189
00:10:01,965 --> 00:10:04,009
Unfortunately, Bret was called out
of the country this morning.

190
00:10:04,217 --> 00:10:05,260
Convenient.

191
00:10:05,385 --> 00:10:07,304
But he asked me to answer
any question you have.

192
00:10:07,888 --> 00:10:09,222
Won't you come this way?
There's tea.

193
00:10:12,142 --> 00:10:13,894
Lester Bradovich
belonged to our church.

194
00:10:14,352 --> 00:10:15,395
I believe there's a good chance

195
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:18,231
that all three of the bodies you found
were church members.

196
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:21,568
- Well, what makes you say that, Mr. Cooper?
- These murders occurred in 1988.

197
00:10:22,277 --> 00:10:24,529
At that time, Visualize
owned the Elliston farm.

198
00:10:24,905 --> 00:10:26,698
Everyone who worked there
was a member of the church.

199
00:10:26,907 --> 00:10:28,366
Why did Bret Stiles
own a farm?

200
00:10:29,451 --> 00:10:30,494
He had a dream.

201
00:10:31,161 --> 00:10:35,082
He saw Visualize controlling an agricultural
empire based on wheat

202
00:10:35,749 --> 00:10:38,460
but he wanted each of the farms
to be a self-sustaining bio-community.

203
00:10:38,585 --> 00:10:41,088
So they had to raise their own
vegetables and livestock.

204
00:10:41,838 --> 00:10:43,048
It was a grand vision.

205
00:10:43,548 --> 00:10:45,008
The church bought
a half-dozen farms.

206
00:10:45,550 --> 00:10:48,345
Half a dozen? All run by people
that knew nothing about farming?

207
00:10:48,970 --> 00:10:52,182
Let's just say that enthusiasm
was a poor substitute for expertise.

208
00:10:52,724 --> 00:10:54,518
They all failed in a year or two.

209
00:10:55,519 --> 00:10:58,480
Lester Bradovich ran the Elliston farm
with two others...

210
00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:01,483
Martin Talbot
and Allan Charney.

211
00:11:02,234 --> 00:11:03,860
That's what we have on them.

212
00:11:05,862 --> 00:11:08,657
In July of 1988, when we hadn't
had any word for some weeks

213
00:11:08,782 --> 00:11:10,617
we sent people down
to investigate.

214
00:11:11,409 --> 00:11:12,619
They found it empty.

215
00:11:13,078 --> 00:11:15,413
We assumed that Bradovich
and the others had abandoned it.

216
00:11:15,747 --> 00:11:17,874
Why didn't you file a missing
persons report?

217
00:11:18,542 --> 00:11:20,794
They were adults.
We weren't their parents.

218
00:11:21,002 --> 00:11:22,087
We assumed they'd run off.

219
00:11:23,338 --> 00:11:26,800
Besides, we have found,
then and now

220
00:11:27,634 --> 00:11:31,471
that the police rarely take
church complaints seriously

221
00:11:31,638 --> 00:11:32,681
when we do file them.

222
00:11:36,810 --> 00:11:39,271
But we are all horrified
by what happened.

223
00:11:40,063 --> 00:11:42,524
We'll do anything at all
to assist in your investigation.

224
00:11:43,233 --> 00:11:44,276
You have my word.

225
00:11:47,237 --> 00:11:49,406
Martin Talbot was older.
No family.

226
00:11:49,614 --> 00:11:51,032
That explains why nobody
was looking for him.

227
00:11:51,408 --> 00:11:53,201
Allan Charney
had family in Idaho.

228
00:11:53,368 --> 00:11:56,288
They did file a missing person's report
but nothing ever came of it.

229
00:11:56,955 --> 00:11:58,540
There's no other information
on either one of them.

230
00:11:58,665 --> 00:12:01,459
All right, call Charney's family
and let them know what happened.

231
00:12:01,751 --> 00:12:05,297
Visualize is sending down their files
on Elliston farm.

232
00:12:05,630 --> 00:12:07,757
Go through 'em.
There might be something useful.

233
00:12:08,008 --> 00:12:09,050
All right. Will do.

234
00:12:10,427 --> 00:12:14,097
I hate cold cases. No evidence, no witnesses
and where do you start?

235
00:12:14,264 --> 00:12:16,474
Oh, it's not so bad.
You know what you need?

236
00:12:16,641 --> 00:12:18,768
- A viable suspect?
- Big bowl of chili verde.

237
00:12:18,977 --> 00:12:20,228
Come on. Let's go.

238
00:12:34,242 --> 00:12:35,285
Ella's Diner?

239
00:12:35,619 --> 00:12:37,787
Best damn chili in town,
I'm told.

240
00:12:37,996 --> 00:12:39,539
Please tell me
we're not here for chili.

241
00:12:40,290 --> 00:12:42,500
Of course not.
We're here to find a killer.

242
00:12:49,150 --> 00:12:51,777
- What are we doing here, Jane?
- Look around.

243
00:12:52,767 --> 00:12:56,354
The collective memory of this entire town
is in this room.

244
00:12:57,063 --> 00:13:00,608
Anything that happened in the last 50 years
you'll find out about it here.

245
00:13:00,942 --> 00:13:02,443
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

246
00:13:02,735 --> 00:13:04,737
- Lemon for your tea?
- No, thanks.

247
00:13:04,904 --> 00:13:06,197
Ma'am, we're with the CBI.

248
00:13:06,489 --> 00:13:08,157
We're investigating some murders

249
00:13:08,324 --> 00:13:10,326
that took place in town
20 years back.

250
00:13:10,451 --> 00:13:11,828
You mean the three bodies
in the barn?

251
00:13:12,203 --> 00:13:13,246
Yeah, I heard.

252
00:13:13,788 --> 00:13:16,082
The victims were from
a group called Visualize

253
00:13:16,207 --> 00:13:18,251
that owned the farm.
Do you know anything about that?

254
00:13:18,459 --> 00:13:20,837
All I know is that
they found three dead guys.

255
00:13:21,796 --> 00:13:23,881
- Can I get you anything else?
- No, thanks.

256
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:26,634
- Collective memory?
- If I may...

257
00:13:26,843 --> 00:13:28,469
who told you about
the three dead guys?

258
00:13:29,053 --> 00:13:31,180
- Everybody knows.
- Yes, but who told you?

259
00:13:41,691 --> 00:13:43,318
Look, I don't wanna
get anybody in trouble.

260
00:13:43,693 --> 00:13:45,069
Oh, of course. Thank you.

261
00:13:51,034 --> 00:13:53,995
Patrick Jane. I'm with the CBI.
What's your name?

262
00:13:54,245 --> 00:13:55,913
Miles MacCambridge. Why?

263
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:57,915
- This is my colleague, Teresa Lisbon.
- Hi.

264
00:13:58,249 --> 00:14:00,626
- You're the town gossip, I take it?
- I am?

265
00:14:02,086 --> 00:14:03,838
Retired schoolteacher,
by the looks.

266
00:14:05,048 --> 00:14:06,424
Who's your source
on the murders?

267
00:14:07,175 --> 00:14:08,384
Former student,
I'm guessing?

268
00:14:08,593 --> 00:14:09,761
Am I in some kind of trouble?

269
00:14:10,386 --> 00:14:11,763
- Possibly.
- No, you're not.

270
00:14:11,929 --> 00:14:12,972
Jury's still out.

271
00:14:13,139 --> 00:14:14,474
We're investigating
the murders

272
00:14:14,599 --> 00:14:16,351
and we'd really appreciate it
if you could tell us

273
00:14:16,476 --> 00:14:19,020
anything about that farm
that we found the bodies at.

274
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:22,190
Oh, yeah. Well, it's...it's been
through a lot of hands.

275
00:14:22,482 --> 00:14:23,524
It's not very good land.

276
00:14:23,858 --> 00:14:26,069
No one's worked it
for at least a decade.

277
00:14:26,277 --> 00:14:27,737
What about the group, Visualize?

278
00:14:27,862 --> 00:14:29,864
They owned the farm
back in the mid-'80s?

279
00:14:30,031 --> 00:14:32,575
Sure, I remember them.
They stuck out.

280
00:14:33,034 --> 00:14:34,494
Pretty useless as farm folk.

281
00:14:35,036 --> 00:14:37,205
How did they get along
with the other people in town?

282
00:14:37,455 --> 00:14:39,040
Were there any conflicts
that you remember?

283
00:14:39,332 --> 00:14:41,250
They mostly kept to themselves.

284
00:14:41,918 --> 00:14:43,836
I remember one person
bitching about 'em...

285
00:14:43,961 --> 00:14:45,963
a farmer named Tom Crayhew.

286
00:14:46,672 --> 00:14:48,800
Couldn't tell you what he was upset about
too long ago.

287
00:14:49,175 --> 00:14:51,219
we really appreciate it.
Thank you.

288
00:14:51,594 --> 00:14:54,055
- So I'm not in any trouble?
- Jury's still out.

289
00:14:54,347 --> 00:14:56,349
- You're fine, sir.
- Looky here.

290
00:14:57,475 --> 00:14:59,143
Took me up on
my recommendation, I see?

291
00:15:00,395 --> 00:15:03,064
- Tell Elena to put your check on my tab.
- Aw, that's okay.

292
00:15:03,272 --> 00:15:05,316
Please. It's a long tab.

293
00:15:06,192 --> 00:15:07,235
Enjoy.

294
00:15:08,736 --> 00:15:10,196
You don't like him.

295
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:12,198
- Former student?
- Yes.

296
00:15:12,490 --> 00:15:15,451
Mean kid? Pulled on the girls' pigtails?

297
00:15:16,619 --> 00:15:17,703
He's chief of police

298
00:15:17,829 --> 00:15:20,206
which is a pretty powerful position
around here.

299
00:15:20,790 --> 00:15:21,916
If you'll excuse me.

300
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:29,132
Is this the stuff
from Visualize?

301
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:31,676
Yeah, it's mostly letters
from the farm to Visualize

302
00:15:31,843 --> 00:15:33,511
asking for more help
and more money.

303
00:15:33,886 --> 00:15:36,597
And Bradovich, Charney, and Talbot
were the only full-time workers.

304
00:15:36,722 --> 00:15:39,100
But Visualize was sending
temporary people all the time...

305
00:15:39,392 --> 00:15:41,352
some members,
some potential recruits.

306
00:15:41,811 --> 00:15:43,312
Their names weren't even
recorded most of the time.

307
00:15:43,563 --> 00:15:44,605
Most of the time?

308
00:15:44,730 --> 00:15:46,858
Yeah, there's one lead...
a guy named Peter Dibuono

309
00:15:47,024 --> 00:15:49,569
is listed as working at the farm
in '86 and '87.

310
00:15:49,986 --> 00:15:51,070
He might be able to tell us something.

311
00:15:51,237 --> 00:15:52,280
I reached him on e-mail.

312
00:15:52,405 --> 00:15:53,448
- He's coming in to talk.
- Good.

313
00:15:53,656 --> 00:15:57,034
You and Rigsby go to Elliston.
Talk to a farmer named Tom Crayhew.

314
00:15:57,243 --> 00:15:59,620
Apparently he had some sort of
beef with the Visualize farm.

315
00:16:00,246 --> 00:16:01,289
I'll be back in an hour.

316
00:16:01,414 --> 00:16:02,707
- Where you going?
- Lunch.

317
00:16:04,625 --> 00:16:06,169
No, no. My hat's off.

318
00:16:06,335 --> 00:16:09,422
I only lasted two days as Jane's boss.
He nearly killed me.

319
00:16:09,672 --> 00:16:11,757
So you...you ...clearly
know things that I don't.

320
00:16:12,008 --> 00:16:15,094
- He is not that hard to handle.
- Really? Seriously?

321
00:16:15,219 --> 00:16:17,638
'Cause I know a couple...
thousand people

322
00:16:17,889 --> 00:16:21,392
who would disagree with that.
So... ten years?

323
00:16:21,684 --> 00:16:23,686
People keep on saying that.
I don't know why.

324
00:16:23,811 --> 00:16:25,313
I don't feel any different.

325
00:16:25,730 --> 00:16:27,273
I'm hoping it means
you're ready for a change.

326
00:16:27,648 --> 00:16:29,066
Oh, what is that
supposed to mean?

327
00:16:30,151 --> 00:16:31,194
I'm leaving CBI.

328
00:16:32,195 --> 00:16:34,197
I've lined up some capital.
I'm starting my own firm.

329
00:16:34,489 --> 00:16:38,159
Security and investigations
for high-end clients.

330
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:41,037
I was wondering if you'd consider
coming with me.

331
00:16:41,537 --> 00:16:42,580
Right.

332
00:16:42,705 --> 00:16:44,415
You're one of the top agents in the division.
Everyone knows it.

333
00:16:44,582 --> 00:16:47,293
You... you deserve a position
commensurate with your gifts.

334
00:16:47,752 --> 00:16:50,922
- I am flattered, Ray, but I like my job.
- Yeah, I like my job, too.

335
00:16:51,047 --> 00:16:53,341
I'd like it better if I'd had a raise
in the past three years.

336
00:16:54,091 --> 00:16:56,010
California's broke,
if you didn't notice.

337
00:16:56,135 --> 00:16:57,512
It's not about the money.

338
00:16:59,931 --> 00:17:01,057
That's the starting salary.

339
00:17:04,769 --> 00:17:06,312
That'll keep you in
leather jackets, I think.

340
00:17:08,898 --> 00:17:12,235
It's not about the money.
I like what I do.

341
00:17:12,485 --> 00:17:14,028
It's a good job. It's good work.

342
00:17:15,112 --> 00:17:17,949
- Is it the work, or is it Jane?
- He's part of the work.

343
00:17:18,533 --> 00:17:21,327
Sure. And you guys...you guys
have a track record

344
00:17:21,536 --> 00:17:23,329
I-I understand why
you wanna stick with that.

345
00:17:23,579 --> 00:17:26,290
But if there's one thing
that I've learned about Jane

346
00:17:26,499 --> 00:17:28,626
it's that catching bad guys
is a game to him

347
00:17:29,001 --> 00:17:30,336
and...and he's gonna
quit someday.

348
00:17:30,753 --> 00:17:32,004
Maybe because he gets
Red John

349
00:17:32,171 --> 00:17:34,090
maybe because...what...
he just gets bored.

350
00:17:34,215 --> 00:17:35,967
When that happens,
you're gonna be on your own.

351
00:17:37,593 --> 00:17:40,972
Driving out to some crime scene
in the middle of nowhere at 5:00 a.m.

352
00:17:41,138 --> 00:17:45,851
asking Rigsby what sort of sad story
you gotta unravel this time.

353
00:17:49,647 --> 00:17:50,690
Is that what you really want?

354
00:17:54,151 --> 00:17:55,528
Because I think
you deserve better.

355
00:17:56,571 --> 00:17:57,613
Think about it.

356
00:18:12,461 --> 00:18:13,504
Mr. Crayhew?

357
00:18:19,010 --> 00:18:21,470
Mr. Crayhew, we're with CBI.
Open up, please!

358
00:18:22,054 --> 00:18:23,973
- I will not be bullied!
- Put the gun down.

359
00:18:24,932 --> 00:18:26,934
You cannot make a man
render unto Caesar

360
00:18:27,268 --> 00:18:28,936
that which he does not
choose to render.

361
00:18:29,353 --> 00:18:31,230
- What?
- Sir, whatever the problem is

362
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,482
we can work it out.
Just put the gun away.

363
00:18:33,733 --> 00:18:36,736
Federal income tax is an illegal taking
under the Fifth Amendment

364
00:18:36,944 --> 00:18:39,572
of the United States Constitution
and I will not pay it!

365
00:18:39,739 --> 00:18:41,616
- Sir, we're not with the I.R.S.
- Says you.

366
00:18:41,866 --> 00:18:43,242
We have nothing to do
with your taxes.

367
00:18:43,492 --> 00:18:45,870
We wanna talk about some killings
at a nearby farm.

368
00:18:47,496 --> 00:18:48,789
You're not federal agents?

369
00:18:50,625 --> 00:18:51,667
You're not here to collect?

370
00:18:51,792 --> 00:18:53,419
No, sir. California Bureau
of Investigation.

371
00:18:54,837 --> 00:18:56,422
Now put the gun down.
Do it now.

372
00:19:00,551 --> 00:19:03,554
Yeah, I remember
those clowns from Visualize.

373
00:19:04,096 --> 00:19:05,890
That land's right next to mine.

374
00:19:06,432 --> 00:19:08,142
Killed everything they... they touched.

375
00:19:08,643 --> 00:19:10,353
- Plus they were thieves.
- Why thieves?

376
00:19:11,354 --> 00:19:13,147
Well, they stole my damn water.

377
00:19:14,148 --> 00:19:15,566
I got a stream up that way.

378
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:19,278
They diverted it into their fields
like I wouldn't notice.

379
00:19:19,862 --> 00:19:24,033
So me and my 12-gauge went
to talk it over with them.

380
00:19:25,326 --> 00:19:28,287
I told those wackadoodles
to stop stealing my water

381
00:19:28,537 --> 00:19:30,039
and one of 'em
pulled a revolver.

382
00:19:30,331 --> 00:19:32,124
I thought we were gonna
have a shooting right there.

383
00:19:32,291 --> 00:19:33,751
Do you recall
what kind of revolver it was?

384
00:19:33,876 --> 00:19:34,919
Nope.

385
00:19:35,544 --> 00:19:38,464
Was one of these...
the man that threatened you?

386
00:19:45,638 --> 00:19:46,681
It was that one.

387
00:19:47,723 --> 00:19:52,144
He was wound up.
Had that look, you know?

388
00:19:53,104 --> 00:19:54,146
Just ready to go.

389
00:19:54,271 --> 00:19:56,524
I truly thought I was gonna have
to shoot him right there.

390
00:19:57,316 --> 00:19:58,359
But I didn't.

391
00:19:59,777 --> 00:20:01,529
I didn't.
I swear to God.

392
00:20:03,823 --> 00:20:06,450
But I don't condemn
whoever did shoot him, though.

393
00:20:07,368 --> 00:20:08,953
You've heard how
they treated their animals?

394
00:20:09,662 --> 00:20:10,705
No. How?

395
00:20:11,122 --> 00:20:13,708
They... tortured 'em, basically.

396
00:20:15,459 --> 00:20:16,502
Unnatural stuff.

397
00:20:17,670 --> 00:20:20,715
- Satanic ritual stuff.
- Really? You know that for a fact?

398
00:20:21,424 --> 00:20:23,300
If you don't believe me, talk to the Vet.

399
00:20:23,801 --> 00:20:26,804
She was on 'em all the time about
how they were treating their stock.

400
00:20:28,222 --> 00:20:29,640
She was ready to call
the county on 'em.

401
00:20:30,349 --> 00:20:33,227
- And what's this vet's name?
- I think she retired now.

402
00:20:35,813 --> 00:20:37,565
Preston. That's it.

403
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:45,823
"Hello, Lisbon."

404
00:20:45,948 --> 00:20:47,658
Are you ever gonna
let me in there again?

405
00:20:48,534 --> 00:20:51,996
- "How was lunch?"
- Lovely. Haffner offered me a job.

406
00:20:56,208 --> 00:20:57,251
Really?

407
00:20:57,710 --> 00:21:00,254
- You gonna take it?
- Right now I'm considering it.

408
00:21:00,463 --> 00:21:01,964
Come on. We have to go talk to the Vet.

409
00:21:04,717 --> 00:21:05,760
Oh, my God.

410
00:21:18,731 --> 00:21:20,065
This one's for Doug.

411
00:21:21,317 --> 00:21:24,028
- Dr. Ellen Preston?
- Oh, no, I'm...

412
00:21:24,403 --> 00:21:27,156
Dr. Preston's my mom.
I'm Holly Preston.

413
00:21:27,364 --> 00:21:28,824
Holly. Pretty good
with those dogs.

414
00:21:29,241 --> 00:21:31,452
Oh, well, thanks.
I should be.

415
00:21:31,577 --> 00:21:33,788
I'm with them all day.
I run a doggy day care.

416
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:37,124
- Is there something I can do for you?
- We're with the CBI.

417
00:21:37,291 --> 00:21:39,335
We need to ask your mother
some questions.

418
00:21:40,961 --> 00:21:42,463
Is that a problem?

419
00:21:44,215 --> 00:21:47,468
She was diagnosed with Alzheimer's
about eight years ago.

420
00:21:48,469 --> 00:21:49,929
She still responds

421
00:21:50,054 --> 00:21:52,181
but she's not very good at talking
to people anymore.

422
00:21:52,848 --> 00:21:55,017
- I'm sorry.
- Oh, don't be.

423
00:21:55,810 --> 00:21:56,852
She looks peaceful.

424
00:21:57,102 --> 00:21:58,979
She is... mostly.

425
00:21:59,230 --> 00:22:00,272
You've probably heard

426
00:22:00,397 --> 00:22:02,358
we found three bodies
on a nearby farm.

427
00:22:02,608 --> 00:22:05,820
25 years ago, the farm was owned
by a church called Visualize.

428
00:22:06,111 --> 00:22:07,363
Does that ring a bell?

429
00:22:07,822 --> 00:22:10,241
I've heard about them.
They made kind of a splash.

430
00:22:10,699 --> 00:22:12,326
Hi, little boy.
Who's a good boy?

431
00:22:12,743 --> 00:22:15,496
Yeah. Oh, he likes that.
You like that?

432
00:22:15,621 --> 00:22:18,165
Is... that what you wanted to
talk to my mom about?

433
00:22:18,332 --> 00:22:19,667
We heard that she was concerned

434
00:22:19,792 --> 00:22:21,877
about the way some of the animals
were being treat.

435
00:22:22,086 --> 00:22:23,420
We just wanted to know
what that was about.

436
00:22:23,712 --> 00:22:26,549
Rumor has it they were
performing satanic rituals.

437
00:22:27,383 --> 00:22:28,425
What?

438
00:22:28,634 --> 00:22:32,930
We'd heard there may have been
some unnatural acts going on.

439
00:22:33,222 --> 00:22:35,224
Have you been talking
to Tom Crayhew?

440
00:22:35,641 --> 00:22:38,644
- Why? Does it sound like him?
- Oh, exactly like him.

441
00:22:39,603 --> 00:22:42,648
I would remember if my mom
thought something was happening.

442
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:44,191
There were no rituals.

443
00:22:44,733 --> 00:22:48,070
I think my mom was just concerned
that the farm was failing.

444
00:22:48,737 --> 00:22:51,282
People take it out on the animals
once a place goes south.

445
00:22:51,448 --> 00:22:52,741
- Did you ever go up there?
- No.

446
00:22:52,950 --> 00:22:56,203
I was 16, and I was
all about boys then.

447
00:22:57,037 --> 00:22:59,373
I wouldn't go to the farm with my mom
if I could avoid it.

448
00:22:59,623 --> 00:23:01,125
Well, all the academic achievements

449
00:23:01,250 --> 00:23:03,711
suggest you were focused
on a little more than just boys.

450
00:23:04,253 --> 00:23:08,465
Well, maybe now and then
but that was a long time ago.

451
00:23:10,259 --> 00:23:12,678
Is that all you wanted to ask about?

452
00:23:13,137 --> 00:23:14,972
- Yes.
- Yeah. Thank you for your time.

453
00:23:15,389 --> 00:23:17,016
- Jane. Jane.
- Yes. Who's that, huh?

454
00:23:17,391 --> 00:23:20,185
Is that good?
That's really good.

455
00:23:20,477 --> 00:23:22,396
- Jane?
- That's really good.

456
00:23:28,193 --> 00:23:29,904
- Agent Van Pelt?
- Yes?

457
00:23:30,112 --> 00:23:32,448
Father Peter Dibuono.
You asked to see me?

458
00:23:32,740 --> 00:23:35,784
Obviously, I haven't been a member
of Visualize for some time.

459
00:23:36,952 --> 00:23:39,496
If I were to be kind to my younger self

460
00:23:39,622 --> 00:23:41,248
I would say that my time with them

461
00:23:41,373 --> 00:23:44,752
was an early stage
in a long spiritual journey.

462
00:23:45,252 --> 00:23:46,545
What if you
weren't being kind?

463
00:23:46,670 --> 00:23:48,547
I was a stupid punk
who didn't know any better.

464
00:23:49,590 --> 00:23:52,009
Bret Stiles says a lot
of things that sound good

465
00:23:52,217 --> 00:23:53,844
if you don't think
about 'em too hard.

466
00:23:54,303 --> 00:23:55,763
Some of what he says
makes sense, though.

467
00:23:55,930 --> 00:23:58,849
Yes. And there's a core of truth
in every good lie.

468
00:24:00,684 --> 00:24:04,772
All that talk about self-actualization
can be very, very seductive.

469
00:24:05,022 --> 00:24:06,440
Oh, it's not just seduction.

470
00:24:07,066 --> 00:24:09,860
What's wrong with wanting to be the most
powerful version of you you can be?

471
00:24:10,402 --> 00:24:11,445
If you were a good person

472
00:24:11,570 --> 00:24:13,197
it would just make you
an even better one.

473
00:24:13,489 --> 00:24:15,032
Well, it depends on how
you look at it, I guess.

474
00:24:16,825 --> 00:24:19,078
You wanted to ask me
about the Elliston farm?

475
00:24:19,703 --> 00:24:21,830
- You worked there, right?
- No, I ran it.

476
00:24:22,206 --> 00:24:23,791
From mid-'86 to late '87.

477
00:24:24,249 --> 00:24:26,168
That was a crazy time.

478
00:24:26,460 --> 00:24:29,004
There was a lot of pressure from Visualize
to make that place work.

479
00:24:29,171 --> 00:24:32,007
We just didn't have the people
or the expertise.

480
00:24:32,841 --> 00:24:35,302
And that's why they brought
Bradovich into take over.

481
00:24:35,636 --> 00:24:36,720
Lester Bradovich?

482
00:24:36,845 --> 00:24:39,765
And Lester... he was a true believer.

483
00:24:40,975 --> 00:24:43,352
He worked people insanely hard...
18, 20 hours a day.

484
00:24:43,477 --> 00:24:46,647
No one lasted long except for him,
Talbot and Charney.

485
00:24:48,440 --> 00:24:52,486
After I left, um, I heard...
this is just a rumor...

486
00:24:53,362 --> 00:24:55,155
that...that Lester had been
giving people drugs

487
00:24:55,614 --> 00:24:57,491
to keep them working
when they were tired.

488
00:24:57,741 --> 00:24:59,243
- What kind of drugs?
- I don't know. I don't know.

489
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:01,954
Speed, I would guess.

490
00:25:02,746 --> 00:25:03,872
But he must have been desperate

491
00:25:04,206 --> 00:25:07,584
because there were so many ex-addicts
in the church at that time

492
00:25:07,710 --> 00:25:10,713
that Visualize
was very strict about drugs.

493
00:25:10,879 --> 00:25:13,757
If he couldn't get the drugs from the church
where would he get 'em?

494
00:25:14,550 --> 00:25:15,926
I have no idea.

495
00:25:17,803 --> 00:25:20,681
Father Dibuono.
Patrick Jane.

496
00:25:20,806 --> 00:25:23,559
Agent Van Pelt told me you used to work
on the Elliston farm.

497
00:25:23,809 --> 00:25:25,894
- That's right.
- May I show you something?

498
00:25:27,104 --> 00:25:28,147
Do you know what that is?

499
00:25:29,314 --> 00:25:32,151
Sure.
Yeah, I saw that.

500
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:34,403
When?

501
00:25:34,820 --> 00:25:38,407
A few months after I left the farm
I, I went back for a visit

502
00:25:39,533 --> 00:25:40,576
and that's when I saw it.

503
00:25:40,951 --> 00:25:43,829
Talbot said that some kid who
worked on the farm... he did it.

504
00:25:43,996 --> 00:25:45,497
"Some kid," that's all
he said about it?

505
00:25:45,873 --> 00:25:47,374
Yeah, yeah. Some kid.

506
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:49,209
How old was Talbot?

507
00:25:50,085 --> 00:25:51,295
50-something?

508
00:25:52,671 --> 00:25:56,925
So anyone under the age
of 30 could be "some kid".

509
00:25:58,385 --> 00:26:01,138
You know, I asked Talbot what it meant
the smiley face.

510
00:26:01,346 --> 00:26:03,557
He said, "what does anything mean?"

511
00:26:04,683 --> 00:26:08,020
But he said it with this really deranged
expression on his face.

512
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:11,857
There was a bad vibe
about that place.

513
00:26:13,233 --> 00:26:14,276
Evil.

514
00:26:16,195 --> 00:26:18,155
I didn't spend more
than half an hour there

515
00:26:19,239 --> 00:26:20,282
and I never went back.

516
00:26:20,407 --> 00:26:24,036
And you've never seen that image
anywhere else ever since?

517
00:26:24,203 --> 00:26:27,206
No. No. But I've been out
of the country a lot.

518
00:26:27,664 --> 00:26:29,666
Missionary work.
Central America.

519
00:26:32,878 --> 00:26:34,338
Is something the matter,
Mr. Jane?

520
00:26:34,546 --> 00:26:36,757
No. No, not at all.
Appreciate your time.

521
00:26:52,312 --> 00:26:53,355
When the murders occurred

522
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:56,233
Bradovich was pushing the farm workers
as hard as possible.

523
00:26:56,817 --> 00:26:58,652
They were stealing water
from neighboring farms

524
00:26:58,819 --> 00:27:00,570
and still failing
to make the farm produce.

525
00:27:01,029 --> 00:27:02,739
How does any of that
get the three of them killed?

526
00:27:02,948 --> 00:27:03,990
Well, I checked on Crayhew.

527
00:27:04,116 --> 00:27:06,118
He spent some time with a right-wing
militia in the mid-'80s.

528
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:08,995
The county sheriff still thinks
he's got a lot more guns on that farm.

529
00:27:09,121 --> 00:27:10,288
It wouldn't surprise me.

530
00:27:10,622 --> 00:27:12,499
What about the forensics team
that was searching the farm?

531
00:27:12,666 --> 00:27:13,750
Did they come up with anything?

532
00:27:13,959 --> 00:27:16,128
- Nothing yet. They're still looking.
- Stay on 'em.

533
00:27:16,628 --> 00:27:18,797
I'm interested in the drugs
the good father mentioned.

534
00:27:19,214 --> 00:27:20,423
He said it was just a rumor.

535
00:27:20,757 --> 00:27:22,634
Well, if Bradovich is working
them around the clock

536
00:27:23,343 --> 00:27:25,262
I'm betting they had
a little chemical help.

537
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:28,223
And if they didn't get it from Visualize
then where did they get it from?

538
00:27:29,307 --> 00:27:31,434
Who knows?
Everyone who could tell us is dead.

539
00:27:32,686 --> 00:27:33,728
Not everyone.

540
00:27:37,190 --> 00:27:38,233
Nope.

541
00:27:38,525 --> 00:27:39,609
Don't remember any of 'em.

542
00:27:40,443 --> 00:27:43,196
Of course, I was just...
17 at the time.

543
00:27:43,989 --> 00:27:45,991
Not likely to run
into boys like these.

544
00:27:46,741 --> 00:27:51,705
And, as far as drug deals in 1988
I couldn't tell you anything about that.

545
00:27:52,497 --> 00:27:54,666
Wasn't bustin' drug dealers quite yet.

546
00:27:56,042 --> 00:27:58,962
Would your office have any record
of drug arrests back then?

547
00:27:59,588 --> 00:28:01,548
A lot of the old files
have been archived.

548
00:28:02,132 --> 00:28:04,259
They're in some warehouse in Fresno.

549
00:28:05,010 --> 00:28:07,762
I can send somebody to go look
but it's gonna... it's gonna take a while.

550
00:28:08,638 --> 00:28:09,681
Could do that.

551
00:28:09,806 --> 00:28:12,184
Or you could just tell us
what drugs you sold 'em.

552
00:28:12,726 --> 00:28:13,768
Excuse me?

553
00:28:13,894 --> 00:28:15,353
You were dealing drugs
back then, weren't you?

554
00:28:15,562 --> 00:28:16,605
And they were clients.

555
00:28:16,771 --> 00:28:18,899
Whatever gives you the idea
that I dealt drugs?

556
00:28:19,274 --> 00:28:21,776
You're self-indulgent.
You're corrupt.

557
00:28:22,152 --> 00:28:23,987
You pretended
not to know Bradovich

558
00:28:24,112 --> 00:28:25,739
when clearly,
you recognize him.

559
00:28:26,198 --> 00:28:27,574
You knew we were gonna
talk about drugs.

560
00:28:27,782 --> 00:28:29,868
So why lie about not selling 'em?

561
00:28:30,035 --> 00:28:31,620
Look, I'm...I'm not
sitting still for this, okay?

562
00:28:31,870 --> 00:28:34,331
You withhold information from us
during this investigation

563
00:28:34,456 --> 00:28:36,333
it's considered obstruction of justice.

564
00:28:36,791 --> 00:28:38,001
They'll take your badge for that.

565
00:28:38,168 --> 00:28:40,212
You might have to start paying
for that chili verde.

566
00:28:48,428 --> 00:28:50,180
I might have dealt
a little in high school.

567
00:28:52,140 --> 00:28:54,142
Lot of people did, okay?
It wasn't no big deal.

568
00:28:55,268 --> 00:28:58,939
And by the way, I'm pretty sure the statute
of limitations has expired on that.

569
00:28:59,731 --> 00:29:01,274
Did you sell drugs to Bradovich?

570
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:06,321
This is between us, right?
Law officer to law officer?

571
00:29:06,529 --> 00:29:07,572
Chief...

572
00:29:10,242 --> 00:29:13,245
He was looking for Speed
to keep his people working.

573
00:29:13,995 --> 00:29:15,038
I sold him some.

574
00:29:16,539 --> 00:29:18,124
But right away,
he came back for more.

575
00:29:18,583 --> 00:29:19,709
A lot more.

576
00:29:21,127 --> 00:29:22,671
I didn't have the kind
of quantity he wanted

577
00:29:22,796 --> 00:29:26,675
so I put him in touch with some
serious guys I knew in Stockton.

578
00:29:27,968 --> 00:29:29,010
Scary dudes.

579
00:29:29,511 --> 00:29:30,679
Hell, maybe they killed 'em.

580
00:29:33,682 --> 00:29:35,225
I'm gonna need those names.

581
00:29:43,525 --> 00:29:46,027
Two of the drug dealers
Anaya gave us are dead.

582
00:29:46,152 --> 00:29:47,612
The other one's
on death row in Texas.

583
00:29:47,862 --> 00:29:49,406
I've got a call in to the Department
of Corrections

584
00:29:49,614 --> 00:29:50,657
to see if I can interview him.

585
00:29:50,991 --> 00:29:54,369
Oh, remember the missing bullet
from the gun we found near the bodies?

586
00:29:55,245 --> 00:29:56,329
Techs finally found it.

587
00:29:56,579 --> 00:29:58,623
It was buried in the dirt
in front of the barn.

588
00:29:58,915 --> 00:30:00,250
Like somebody
had shot into the ground.

589
00:30:00,417 --> 00:30:01,459
Where outside the barn?

590
00:30:01,710 --> 00:30:03,253
Right in front
of the big painted face.

591
00:30:04,879 --> 00:30:05,922
So what do you think?

592
00:30:06,047 --> 00:30:07,757
Should I go to Texas
and talk to this guy?

593
00:30:07,924 --> 00:30:10,135
By all means, take a trek
to the Lone Star state.

594
00:30:10,427 --> 00:30:12,929
But I think I can solve
this case quicker.

595
00:30:13,221 --> 00:30:14,264
How?

596
00:30:14,389 --> 00:30:16,725
Get everyone we've interviewed
to Ella's Diner

597
00:30:16,975 --> 00:30:18,101
first thing tomorrow morning.

598
00:30:18,268 --> 00:30:20,520
- Well, why the diner?
- For the coffee, of course.

599
00:30:27,986 --> 00:30:29,029
Hey, Ray.

600
00:30:29,195 --> 00:30:31,448
Hey. I was, just checking in.

601
00:30:31,573 --> 00:30:33,074
Seeing if you had a chance
to think about the offer.

602
00:30:33,325 --> 00:30:34,951
I think you already know my answer.

603
00:30:36,161 --> 00:30:38,830
- You're missing out on something big.
- I'm sure I am.

604
00:30:39,497 --> 00:30:40,665
How's the case?
You getting anywhere?

605
00:30:41,041 --> 00:30:42,083
We're making progress.

606
00:30:42,208 --> 00:30:43,626
Did you get anything from
the stuff Cooper sent down?

607
00:30:43,752 --> 00:30:45,295
No, not much.

608
00:30:49,466 --> 00:30:53,178
Ray, Jason Cooper's name
isn't on that board.

609
00:30:53,803 --> 00:30:54,846
So?

610
00:30:56,056 --> 00:30:57,932
How did you know
that he sent us material?

611
00:30:59,601 --> 00:31:00,852
Someone on your team
must have told me.

612
00:31:01,144 --> 00:31:03,188
No one else knew
except myself and Jane.

613
00:31:06,733 --> 00:31:08,610
Did somebody from
the church tell you?

614
00:31:11,071 --> 00:31:12,113
Ray?

615
00:31:12,614 --> 00:31:13,865
Are you a member of Visualize?

616
00:31:16,493 --> 00:31:17,660
As a matter of fact, I am.

617
00:31:19,454 --> 00:31:22,123
I don't talk about it much.
People tend to take it the wrong way...

618
00:31:23,083 --> 00:31:25,043
the way that you're taking it
right now, I think.

619
00:31:25,210 --> 00:31:26,544
- Oh, that's not true.
- Really?

620
00:31:27,253 --> 00:31:28,296
No.

621
00:31:30,632 --> 00:31:32,509
Look, I was a screwed-up kid.

622
00:31:32,842 --> 00:31:34,219
I was headed down a bad path

623
00:31:34,344 --> 00:31:36,763
and some people from Visualize
took an interest

624
00:31:37,514 --> 00:31:39,057
and helped straighten me out.

625
00:31:39,516 --> 00:31:42,685
I don't think that Bret Stiles is God.

626
00:31:42,811 --> 00:31:45,563
You know, he just happens to say
some things that I believe.

627
00:31:46,356 --> 00:31:49,818
The money to fund your agency...
is that coming from Visualize?

628
00:31:50,026 --> 00:31:51,444
Some, not all.

629
00:31:53,196 --> 00:31:56,366
Teresa, stop looking at me
like I just turned into a robot.

630
00:31:56,741 --> 00:31:57,784
It's just me.

631
00:33:18,446 --> 00:33:21,365
- Just in time, Lisbon.
- Morning, Jane. Nice party.

632
00:33:21,657 --> 00:33:24,035
- Can I get some of that coffee?
- Nope.

633
00:33:24,452 --> 00:33:26,162
This is only for the suspects.

634
00:33:26,787 --> 00:33:27,830
- What?
- For who?

635
00:33:27,955 --> 00:33:29,332
- What did you say?
- That's right.

636
00:33:29,582 --> 00:33:32,627
You're all here because one of you
killed Lester Bradovich

637
00:33:33,336 --> 00:33:35,338
Martin Talbot, and Allen Charney.

638
00:33:36,714 --> 00:33:39,175
Why would I kill three members
of my own church?

639
00:33:39,467 --> 00:33:42,386
Well, because the farm was failing
and Bret Stiles didn't want that to happen.

640
00:33:42,512 --> 00:33:43,846
But maybe it wasn't you.

641
00:33:44,263 --> 00:33:48,184
Mr. Bradovich here was angry with his brother
for abandoning his family.

642
00:33:48,476 --> 00:33:51,312
Even if I was, why would I kill
the other two guys?

643
00:33:51,521 --> 00:33:53,481
Who knows how a homicidal maniac thinks?

644
00:33:53,606 --> 00:33:57,610
- Homicidal maniac?
- I was 16, Mr. Jane.

645
00:33:58,027 --> 00:34:00,988
Yes, but your mother was an adult
and she was angry with the farmers

646
00:34:01,113 --> 00:34:02,949
over how they were
treating the animals.

647
00:34:03,157 --> 00:34:06,494
Are you seriously saying
my mother killed these people?

648
00:34:06,827 --> 00:34:08,246
You're right.
Maybe it was the priest.

649
00:34:08,538 --> 00:34:11,541
He was angry because he was displaced
as the farm's leader.

650
00:34:11,749 --> 00:34:12,792
No, I wasn't.

651
00:34:12,917 --> 00:34:15,586
Then there's the angry farmer
and the police chief.

652
00:34:16,128 --> 00:34:19,173
Whoever did this,
it was definitely one of you

653
00:34:19,298 --> 00:34:21,050
and in a moment,
we're gonna find out which one.

654
00:34:21,384 --> 00:34:22,426
But first...

655
00:34:23,386 --> 00:34:24,845
more coffee, anyone?

656
00:34:27,139 --> 00:34:30,977
- No. No. No. No. No.
- Mom. Mom.

657
00:34:31,102 --> 00:34:32,478
- No, no, no.
- Mom...

658
00:34:32,895 --> 00:34:34,021
Mom, take it easy.

659
00:34:34,397 --> 00:34:35,898
What's her problem?
Why's she so upset?

660
00:34:36,023 --> 00:34:38,901
She recognizes the symbol.
There was one at the farm.

661
00:34:39,151 --> 00:34:40,987
- You mean the face on the barn?
- Right.

662
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:42,989
And she knows
what happened underneath it.

663
00:34:43,155 --> 00:34:44,198
Are you saying she's the killer?

664
00:34:44,323 --> 00:34:46,117
We're gonna have to ask your mother
some questions.

665
00:34:46,242 --> 00:34:48,578
- No. You can't.
- Ms. Preston, you got no choice.

666
00:34:48,828 --> 00:34:49,870
She won't understand.

667
00:34:49,996 --> 00:34:51,497
Well, we're gonna have to try.

668
00:34:51,622 --> 00:34:53,916
- Come on. We'll treat her right.
- No. It's not her.

669
00:34:54,250 --> 00:34:55,293
It's me.

670
00:34:58,379 --> 00:34:59,422
It's me.

671
00:35:01,757 --> 00:35:04,093
You thought it was me
when we met, didn't you?

672
00:35:04,594 --> 00:35:06,137
I thought that was a possibility.

673
00:35:06,804 --> 00:35:09,974
Young woman, high-achieving
ends up running a doggy day care.

674
00:35:10,641 --> 00:35:13,269
Suggested something went wrong
along the way.

675
00:35:14,061 --> 00:35:15,104
Yeah.

676
00:35:16,022 --> 00:35:17,064
That sounds about right.

677
00:35:21,861 --> 00:35:25,656
My mother was concerned
about the Visualize farm.

678
00:35:27,033 --> 00:35:31,203
I was riding along when she made
a visit in the area.

679
00:35:32,538 --> 00:35:34,749
"And she said
she wanted to check on them"

680
00:35:35,207 --> 00:35:36,584
"to see what was going on."

681
00:35:38,836 --> 00:35:39,962
"It had rained that day..."

682
00:35:41,213 --> 00:35:42,590
"So the ground was wet."

683
00:35:46,844 --> 00:35:49,305
You stay here.
I'll be just a minute.

684
00:35:55,645 --> 00:35:58,022
Next thing I knew,
I heard someone shouting.

685
00:35:58,397 --> 00:36:00,608
- Don't tell me what to do!
- There are laws.

686
00:36:00,733 --> 00:36:02,943
- I don't care about any of your laws!
- Laws that say this animal

687
00:36:03,069 --> 00:36:04,612
has to be fed and taken care of!

688
00:36:04,737 --> 00:36:07,615
And if you won't take care of it,
I will report you!

689
00:36:07,740 --> 00:36:09,492
I... I should...

690
00:36:10,534 --> 00:36:12,745
You want me to take care of it? Fine!
I'll take care of it!

691
00:36:16,749 --> 00:36:20,044
He looked so crazy.
Just out of his mind.

692
00:36:20,628 --> 00:36:22,129
Well, he was amped up on Speed.

693
00:36:22,421 --> 00:36:25,049
He'd been sleep-deprived for weeks.
He was out of his mind.

694
00:36:27,051 --> 00:36:28,094
What happened next?

695
00:36:29,053 --> 00:36:30,137
Oh, you son of a bitch!

696
00:36:33,766 --> 00:36:34,809
Mom!

697
00:36:39,397 --> 00:36:41,565
I thought he was gonna
kill my mother.

698
00:36:42,441 --> 00:36:43,526
I had to do something.

699
00:36:44,777 --> 00:36:45,820
Hey!

700
00:36:45,945 --> 00:36:46,987
Get off her!

701
00:36:47,613 --> 00:36:48,656
Hey!

702
00:36:55,079 --> 00:36:56,330
I-I don't understand...

703
00:36:57,164 --> 00:36:59,667
Where were Charney and Talbot?
How did they get shot?

704
00:37:00,084 --> 00:37:01,127
I don't know.

705
00:37:04,422 --> 00:37:05,464
What?

706
00:37:05,715 --> 00:37:09,093
I-I don't know anything
about them, I swear. I...

707
00:37:10,594 --> 00:37:11,637
It wasn't me.

708
00:37:12,471 --> 00:37:13,514
What wasn't you?

709
00:37:16,100 --> 00:37:17,143
Holly?

710
00:37:18,602 --> 00:37:20,229
What happened
when you hid the body?

711
00:37:23,899 --> 00:37:28,529
My mother was sure I could go to jail.
And she couldn't let that happen.

712
00:37:29,155 --> 00:37:30,948
She wouldn't let that happen.

713
00:37:32,032 --> 00:37:35,661
"She knew there was a basement
in the barn."

714
00:37:36,996 --> 00:37:38,289
"So we went down there"

715
00:37:39,206 --> 00:37:40,875
"and that's when we found
the other two."

716
00:37:44,962 --> 00:37:47,715
Talbot and Charney's bodies
were already there?

717
00:37:49,383 --> 00:37:51,051
I-I guess it was them.

718
00:37:51,177 --> 00:37:52,845
Did you see anything else?
Anything at all?

719
00:37:53,512 --> 00:37:54,555
No.

720
00:37:54,722 --> 00:37:56,140
After we dumped the body

721
00:37:56,474 --> 00:37:58,392
we got out of there
as fast as we could.

722
00:38:00,019 --> 00:38:01,896
"I had a feeling..."

723
00:38:03,314 --> 00:38:06,108
"Someone was there with us."

724
00:38:10,988 --> 00:38:12,406
Someone was watching us.

725
00:38:21,707 --> 00:38:23,834
- Hey. You got a second?
- Sure.

726
00:38:24,919 --> 00:38:29,465
Word is Holly Preston's only gonna face
manslaughter charges for the homicides.

727
00:38:29,882 --> 00:38:30,925
That true?

728
00:38:31,050 --> 00:38:33,219
We suggested it to the D.D.A.,
and he agreed.

729
00:38:33,344 --> 00:38:34,386
Really?

730
00:38:34,512 --> 00:38:37,139
She claims she did it in self-defense
and we believed her.

731
00:38:37,556 --> 00:38:39,099
- She'll serve time.
- Three guys got killed.

732
00:38:39,308 --> 00:38:41,894
She only killed one of them,
and it was in self-defense.

733
00:38:42,144 --> 00:38:43,187
That's what she says.

734
00:38:43,312 --> 00:38:44,897
Are you in the business
of taking killers at their word?

735
00:38:45,147 --> 00:38:46,857
I believe her.

736
00:38:47,399 --> 00:38:49,944
Did someone from Visualize
ask you to talk to me?

737
00:38:50,569 --> 00:38:53,948
Because you've never come to my office
and questioned my arrests before.

738
00:38:54,406 --> 00:38:55,950
Yeah, I might have gotten some calls.

739
00:38:56,492 --> 00:38:59,119
Might've heard some concern
that you pushed for lighter sentences

740
00:38:59,245 --> 00:39:02,581
because the victims were church
and maybe you don't like the church.

741
00:39:03,290 --> 00:39:04,500
Absolutely not.

742
00:39:04,750 --> 00:39:06,377
Would you be willing to push for
a murder charge

743
00:39:06,544 --> 00:39:07,920
have another talk with the D.D.A.?

744
00:39:08,254 --> 00:39:09,672
Absolutely not.

745
00:39:11,757 --> 00:39:13,634
Okay. Had to try.

746
00:39:14,760 --> 00:39:15,970
Thank you for hearing me out.

747
00:39:18,639 --> 00:39:20,182
Hey, I-I heard about Jane's trick.

748
00:39:20,307 --> 00:39:21,934
How did he make that symbol
appear on the table?

749
00:39:22,351 --> 00:39:24,311
Lemon juice.
Kid's invisible ink.

750
00:39:24,603 --> 00:39:26,522
The heat from the coffee pot
made it appear.

751
00:39:26,981 --> 00:39:28,315
Lemon juice. Of course.

752
00:39:30,860 --> 00:39:32,194
Hey, Ray...

753
00:39:32,945 --> 00:39:35,489
You said that you joined Visualize
as a teenager.

754
00:39:35,906 --> 00:39:39,535
- So 1988, you would have been 21?
- Yeah, about that.

755
00:39:39,743 --> 00:39:41,954
Did they ever send you to work
at the Elliston farm?

756
00:39:42,788 --> 00:39:43,831
Were you there?

757
00:39:45,708 --> 00:39:49,753
Lisbon, we're still friends, right?
Why would you ask me that?

758
00:40:04,810 --> 00:40:06,312
- Lisbon.
- Hi, Jane.

759
00:40:07,313 --> 00:40:08,355
What's up?

760
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:10,733
I had something I wanted to tell you
but I've had enough of this.

761
00:40:10,983 --> 00:40:12,610
- Let me in, or else.
- Or else what?

762
00:40:12,860 --> 00:40:14,194
I don't know, but you're not
gonna like it.

763
00:40:16,530 --> 00:40:17,656
Are we partners or what?

764
00:40:20,326 --> 00:40:21,368
Come in, partner.

765
00:40:32,755 --> 00:40:33,797
Wow.

766
00:40:34,548 --> 00:40:35,716
What did you want
to tell me?

767
00:40:40,971 --> 00:40:42,598
Visualize absolutely refuses

768
00:40:42,723 --> 00:40:45,184
to let us look at a membership
list from 1988

769
00:40:45,351 --> 00:40:47,102
or any other year before or after.

770
00:40:47,645 --> 00:40:50,064
And since they're a recognized
religious organization

771
00:40:50,189 --> 00:40:51,649
no judge will give us a warrant.

772
00:40:52,733 --> 00:40:55,277
If Red John was a member
of Visualize in 1988

773
00:40:55,444 --> 00:40:56,862
- we'll never know.
- Figures.

774
00:40:57,863 --> 00:40:58,906
Never mind.

775
00:40:59,657 --> 00:41:02,451
We know that Red John
was at Elliston farm in 1988.

776
00:41:03,619 --> 00:41:05,955
That is a huge leap forward,
Lisbon. Huge.

777
00:41:06,205 --> 00:41:07,247
How so?

778
00:41:09,708 --> 00:41:13,253
I recalled all the names
of the 2,164 people

779
00:41:13,462 --> 00:41:17,925
that I met and shook hands with
since Red John murdered my family.

780
00:41:18,759 --> 00:41:21,136
I might have missed
one or two, but not many.

781
00:41:21,553 --> 00:41:25,307
807 of those names
were women.

782
00:41:25,975 --> 00:41:27,601
And Red John's
probably not a woman.

783
00:41:27,893 --> 00:41:32,022
Another 949 were men
that I had brief encounters with

784
00:41:32,147 --> 00:41:34,024
that I never saw
or heard from again.

785
00:41:34,900 --> 00:41:37,778
That leaves 408 names.

786
00:41:38,487 --> 00:41:41,407
And Elliston farm will exclude
a whole bunch more.

787
00:41:43,951 --> 00:41:47,121
I'm getting close, Lisbon.
I'm getting very close.

788
00:41:47,663 --> 00:41:48,706
Tell me some of the names.

789
00:41:48,831 --> 00:41:50,457
There's gotta be some
that are at the top of the list.

790
00:41:51,041 --> 00:41:52,084
Sure.

791
00:41:52,751 --> 00:41:54,253
There's some
interesting names.

792
00:41:54,837 --> 00:41:55,879
Well, tell me.

